.TH "SPEECHRECOGNITION.SOUNDSTART_EVENT" "3JS" "October 27, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
SpeechRecognition.soundstart_event \- SpeechRecognition: soundstart
event
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]soundstart\f[B]\f[R] event of the Web Speech API is fired
when any sound \(em recognizable speech or not \(em has been detected.
.SH SYNTAX
Use the event name in \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
addEventListener(\(dqsoundstart\(dq, (event) => { })
.EE
.PP
Alternatively, assign to the event handler property of the interface:
.IP
.EX
onsoundstart = (event) => { }
.EE
.SH EVENT TYPE
A generic \f[CR]Event\f[R] with no added properties.
.SH EXAMPLES
You can use the \f[CR]soundstart\f[R] event in an
\f[CR]addEventListener\f[R] method:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] recognition = \f[B]new\f[R] (
  window.SpeechRecognition || window.webkitSpeechRecognition
)();

recognition.addEventListener(\(dqsoundstart\(dq, () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(dqSome sound is being received\(dq);
});
.EE
.PP
Or use the \f[CR]onsoundstart\f[R] event handler property (but note that
this replaces other event listeners added using \f[CR]onsoundstart\f[R],
if any):
.IP
.EX
recognition.onsoundstart = () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(dqSome sound is being received\(dq);
};
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
Web Speech API
